\section{Turn the selector's promises into tests}

Hjort Blindell sets a priority: first implement the behavior of the IR, then seek good assembly \cite[chapter~1]{blindell2016instruction}. Test those promises separately. A faster result with the wrong behavior is not a performance win, and an assignment with a low objective is not necessarily a legal lowering. For a compiler prototype, define the supported IR, target features, and observations before comparing selectors. The observations might include return values, memory effects in their required order, and the presence and kind of a trap; instruction counts and temporary register contents are not semantic observations unless an interface exposes them.

At the matcher interface, check that operand permutations, immediates, types, and feature flags agree with the rule's preconditions. At the cover interface, require that every live operation is accounted for, any allowed duplication is explicit, and all external uses are supplied. Check placement and edge obligations separately, including the sources of \(\phi\)-operands. At emission, check that register classes, ties, clobbers, branch targets, and encodings obey the target contract. Correctness testing should compare the resulting program against an independent interpreter or a reference lowering, not just against another implementation of the same declarative rules. On small instances, exhaustively enumerate legal covers as an oracle for a claim of minimum modeled cost. That oracle checks optimization, not semantics.

