\section{Audit a paired add from matcher through allocation}

A toy byte machine implements four-input, two-output \texttt{PAIRADD8} as two independent wrapping sums, left result first. Inputs \(a,b,c,d\) enter in \texttt{r0} through \texttt{r3}. The return interface requires the ordered results in \texttt{r0,r1}. The pair instruction has fixed outputs \texttt{r4,r5}, both caller-clobbered. Ordinary three-operand \texttt{ADD8} reads its operands before writing its destination and can write the return registers directly. The initial objective charges one per selected add instruction and omits result moves. In this deliberately small example each machine instruction occupies four bytes; the common return is excluded from both counts.

\begin{verbatim}
# Reference: compare ordered results, including the byte wrap.
inputs: (255,1,4,7) -> want (0,11)

# Pair matches the two adds, but allocation must honor the ABI.
PAIRADD8 r4,r5 <- r0,r1,r2,r3
MOV8 r0 <- r4
MOV8 r1 <- r5
check: (0,11), model=1, encoded bytes=3*4=12

# Separate adds can write the return registers directly.
ADD8 r0 <- r0,r1
ADD8 r1 <- r2,r3
check: (0,11), model=2, encoded bytes=2*4=8

# Mutation: swap the sources of the pair's two output moves.
check: (11,0) != (0,11); reject regardless of cost
\end{verbatim}

The scalar sequence reads the original \texttt{r1} before overwriting it. For either lowering, the semantic invariant is that the first result depends on \(a,b\) and appears in \texttt{r0}, while the second depends on \(c,d\) and appears in \texttt{r1}. Exhausting inputs from 0 through 15 checks \(16^4=65{,}536\) combinations of that subset, not all byte values; the explicit 255 case checks a wrap that the subset cannot trigger. Ensure tests force each lowering so a selector that never emits the paired instruction cannot appear to validate it. The model prefers the pair, but the encoded block is four bytes larger. Neither count determines which block takes fewer cycles on a physical implementation.

\Cref{fig:visual-278} traces just the result locations, where an ostensibly cheaper cover picks up extra instructions.

