\section{Account for the gap between score and cost}

Hjort Blindell's discussion of optimality distinguishes the instruction set and representation under consideration from the code quality attained after scheduling and allocation. His conclusion highlights flag lifetimes, register classes and rematerialization as reasons to distrust isolated optima \cite[chapter~6]{blindell2016instruction}. In the example, a move cost can be incorporated once the result locations are known, but a general selector may not know them. Record how much post-selection work came from class transfers, spills, copies and scheduling delays. A mismatch between the score and the executable outcome is evidence about the cost model, not automatically a solver defect. If the model predicts one score and the exhaustive cover oracle finds a lower one under identical constraints, that is a separate result.

Distinguish a verified feasible incumbent from a proven optimum, an incomplete search with no incumbent, and an infeasible model. On a timeout, report the achieved score and the best bound when available; if a fallback was used, time it too. In \emph{Complete and Practical Universal Instruction Selection}, Hjort Blindell et al.\ use timeouts for constraint solving and explain that their exact-cover formulation excludes recomputation. Optimality in that formulation cannot rule out a faster result from a selector that recomputes a value instead of keeping it live. Their evaluation also identifies the target and how medium-sized benchmark functions were sampled, details that matter when interpreting what the results generalize to.

\section{Measure changes as controlled comparisons}

Hold input IR, target options, later passes, and workload constant when comparing candidate selectors. Report the search budget, match coverage, compiler time and peak memory as well as final code size and execution time. Log the target and inputs; for timing, interleave repeated runs with equal work and report uncertainty. Include workloads stressing the advertised advantage and workloads where late copies or scheduling can reverse it. If a new matcher broadens the candidate set, compare it with the baseline on the old shared set as well; that separates better search from better instruction support. Report correctness failures and timeouts alongside performance rather than discarding them. A useful final claim is specific: the candidate preserves the tested contract and improves a named metric on a named workload at a stated compilation cost.
